e $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n$\\exp (\\intg(x)dx)$ {{field-2}}\nwhere $\\exp (x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^{x}$." 13194139533378 17592186045585 true] [17592186217768 1072 #uuid "02a15c04-b231-5e46-af80-834c2e1d6bde" 13194139533378 17592186045585 true] [17592186217769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216456 13194139533378 17592186045585 true] [17592186217769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216460 13194139533378 17592186045585 true] [17592186217769 1069 "q-318660" 13194139533378 17592186045585 true] [17592186217769 1071 "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n$y^{3}dx + xydy = 0, y > 0$\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. For a differential equation of the form\n$P(x, y)dx + Q(x, y)dy = 0$,\nwe have that if\n\n$\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x})$ {{field-1}}\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n$\\exp (\\inth(x)dy)$ {{field-2}}\nwhere $\\exp (y)$ denotes the exponential function $e^{y}$." 13194139533378 17592186045585 true] [17592186217769 1072 #uuid "16348fba-4613-56cd-be81-305ba49c72ee" 13194139533378 17592186045585 true] [17592186217770 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216465 13194139533378 17592186045585 true] [17592186217770 1069 "q-320854" 13194139533378 17592186045585 true] [17592186217770 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n1. $yy^{′} + y^{4} = xy^{2}$\n2. $y^{′} = x^{3}y^{2} + 2xy + x$\n3. $yy^{′} - xy^{3} + 2xy^{2} = y\\sin (x)$" 13194139533378 17592186045585 true] [17592186217770 1072 #uuid "d592cb5a-2a92-56d1-8947-9547c94c5cb1" 13194139533378 17592186045585 true] [17592186217771 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216471 13194139533378 17592186045585 true] [17592186217771 1069 "q-321225" 13194139533378 17592186045585 true] [17592186217771 1071 "Given that $y_{0}(x) = e^{x}$ is a particular solution of the Riccati different